BEAM processes isolate their state. Supervision observes worker failure and applies restart policy. Restart still loses volatile state.

> Shared-nothing concurrency ensures complete isolation between processes: one process can’t compromise the internal state of another.

Source: [Q03](/lore/durability-bibliography#q03); [B1](/lore/durability-bibliography#b1-erlang-and-otp-in-action), [B2](/lore/durability-bibliography#b2-elixir-in-action), [B3](/lore/durability-bibliography#b3-designing-elixir-systems-with-otp).

## Failure it prevents

A failed computation has already changed shared state, while the caller assumes process isolation.

## Our Effect 4.0.0 reading

[Scope](/lore/scopes-own-resources) and `FiberSet` own local lifetimes. They do not supply separate heaps or OTP restart strategies. Use a host boundary for hard isolation.

Source: `packages/effect/src/Scope.ts`, `packages/effect/src/FiberSet.ts`, `packages/effect/src/Fiber.ts`.

## How to check

State which failure the boundary contains. Test a worker [defect](/lore/effect-basics) and a hard runtime exit separately. This check is our proposal. The books do not prescribe it.

Continue with [Supervision chooses what restarts](/lore/supervision-is-a-recovery-policy), [Durable work needs storage](/lore/durable-work-needs-storage).

Connect this to [the existing rat-stack guidance](/lore/concurrent-work-needs-an-owner).

